ocused Isle of the modern  era of Final Fantasy games doesn't feel  like it comes at the expense of the  methodical nature of the series's roots  the hybrid style lets you glide between  characters at the touch of a button and  assume direct control at the same time  commands can be issued to characters our  otherwise acting independently conjuring  the spirit of that deliberate stand in  place and fight former of old the remake  uses an active time bar to govern  special abilities spells and item uses  to encourage juggling of party members  ATB bars feel much more rapidly when you  take control of the character and attack  the enemy directly and characters tend  not to use the more advanced skills of  their own volition so it's super  important that you step in and put their  resources to good use each playable  character also has a unique skill that  comes at no cost and has a great deal of  strategic value characters can also use  various offensive and defensive magical  spells provided they have the materia  that bestows is the ability to them  material was and still is the core of  Final Fantasy sevens gameplay the beauty  of the materia system is that it allows  you to create loadouts in a very  freeform way and build characters to fit  your preferred style or strategy for any  situation although each playable  character has a general archetype  materia system presents a great deal of  fluidity within this so even your  long-range fire can play a support  magician if you want  the demands of moment-to-moment combat  are high especially since enemies can be  vicious if you're not careful they will  poison and paralyze to create openings  for each other make areas of the  battlefield deadly to limit your  movement and pounce on a character to  trap them forcing you to switch  characters to free your ensnare party  member most enemies have some sort of  elemental weakness that can be  identified and then exploit if you do  this then become pressured and  eventually staggered leaving them  completely defenseless enemies can also  interrupt your actions or move out of  the way entirely to evade you so precise  timing is also crucial thankfully when  issuing commands the action slows to a  crawl to give you breathing room this  won't save you if your plan is  ill-considered though so you've got to  be small  on occasion particularly in tightest  spaces the camera can struggle to keep  up with the action but it's not often  enough to be a serious problem as a  whole the combat has the fluidity as  well as the cinematic and visually  stunning flare of modern Final Fantasy  games but also the satisfaction of the  plan your work and work your plan  approach of the classics add-on the  upgrading mechanics which allow you to  spend points on each weapon to bolster  attributes and you've got a robust  interconnected set of RPG mechanics I  can confidently say that Final Fantasy  has never felt this good to play  for a strongest Final Fantasy 7 remake  gameplay is it's the narrative and the  characters that truly stand out as its  crowning achievement for the vast  majority of the game Final Fantasy 7  remake is a focused deeply personal  story even though avalanches ultimate  goal is to free the planet from the  vampiric jaws of Shinra the events that  transpire narrowed that battle down to a  struggle for the here and the now  instead of the future unlike the  original there's also a much greater  emphasis on the moral gray areas of the  bowel avalanche essentially pokes the  sleeping dragon and when Shinra  retaliates  it's the already downtrodden people of  the slums that suffer they live a meager  existence albeit one they're comfortable  with as citizens of the undersea living  in the squalor of homes built from  rusted metal sheets propped up and  forced together is all they've known and  all they've known has been provided by  Shinra because of that many don't see  avalanches fight against Shinra as a  clear-cut battle betwee